<p>A U.S. Air Force KC-135 aerial refuelling aircraft crashed in western Iraq on Thursday while supporting U.S. operations against Iran, U.S. Central Command said. Centcom added that another aircraft involved landed safely. The military said the incident took place in “friendly airspace” during Operation Epic Fury and was not caused by hostile fire or friendly fire. As rescue efforts continued, the U.S. military later confirmed that four people were killed in the crash. The crash comes as the broader U.S.-Israel war with Iran, which multiple outlets say is in its second week, continues.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/0t2ldQeQ5YsJ8x1w_VI9ShHrecLVUvNlyWVpq1V_6sS-4wocIgOmjlljxo-wRNUAe_Dpwmqh9kvM7bSoR2uQj7XsTKATj4SMvnpR6l3aibzffm8-2mkuEx9bD5peURetpoP93Q5bdt_zKG6hTbNDFts5OxSdaHg2i2pNGuIVWwbyUbYR_x3Y9RDBYnZ7Ha5XjuBoC8WYg_l3ArWJ_cTQkaukyMdpzy_NEKIR-K5BH88Ljg2quCa8_8MCLRQb9y6qVLcKmH978ZVI1eV5Mw' alt='File photo of a U.S. Air Force KC-135 Stratotanker taking off from Kadena Air Base, Japan (Aug. 30, 2023).' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Crew reports: A U.S. official told CBS News the crew’s status was initially unknown after the tanker went down in western Iraq.</li><li>Competing claims: The Islamic Resistance in Iraq said it shot down the KC-135 “in defence of our country’s sovereignty and airspace,” a claim that conflicts with Centcom’s statement that the crash was not due to hostile fire.</li><li>War losses: The Hindu said the KC-135 was at least the fourth U.S. military aircraft lost in the conflict, after three U.S. F-15s were shot down by friendly fire over Kuwait. Politico reported that all six crew members from those fighter-jet losses ejected safely.</li><li>Trump remarks: President Donald Trump has offered shifting public assessments of the campaign’s progress in recent days, including saying the war was “very complete” in a CBS interview and later that it was “moving along very rapidly” at a White House event.</li></ul><blockquote>The situation with 